1.3.m:
Wrath is my brother, endowed with great strength and courage;
by the two of us the great universe, both mobile and immobile, has been driven forth;
everything from Brahmā down to a blade of grass, with the moving and the unmoving, has been overwhelmed."

Then the mighty archer went swiftly, having drawn his great bow adorned with flowers;
taking up his charming arrows, the hero, the sole conqueror of the world,
Smara, the foremost among fighters, appeared upon that snowy mountain on earth.

At that very instant, [Śambhu] was quickly pierced by the arrow named Mohana;
being pierced then, Śambhu slowly opened his eyes
and beheld the mountain-born goddess, just as the ocean beholds the crescent of the moon:

Caṇḍī was likewise worshipped, along with the bhūtas, pramathas, and guhyakas,
with garments, ornaments, and jewels of various kinds;
and all others who had arrived there were also worshipped.

Those mountains beginning with Meru were seated by Nandin;
they took part in the bridal procession as prescribed by the Himālaya.
Together with all those kinsmen, they made their return.

Dwelling in his own abode, the Himālaya shone, possessed of great fame,
by means of the supreme splendor born of contact with Śiva alone;
the great mountain became celebrated and renowned in the three worlds.

Having spoken thus, the mighty hero Mucukunda,
even as he struck with his sword, was struck by a spear;
and the son of Māndhātṛ fell there upon the battlefield.

Nandī said:
Indeed, the mountains have been made into the form of liṅgas by you;
how then, O Lord, are these abodes of Śiva to be worshipped by all the deities? 31.96

We wish to hear all that; let it be told accurately.
There is indeed no other in the world better than you among speakers;
therefore tell everything, O brahmin, to us who desire to hear.

Lomaśa said:
In the eighteen Purāṇas, Śiva alone is sung as supreme;
therefore no one is able to utter the māhātmya of Śiva.

