Standing there, Mahādeva did not rise up to greet him;
thereupon Dakṣa, enraged, spoke [these] words:

Mahādeva said:
You ought not to be angry at any time toward brahmins;
for these brahmins are teachers, constantly devoted to Vedic disputation.

Abandoning supreme faith, that lowest of men became intent on censuring the worshippers of Śiva;
approaching there with all the great seers, he reviled the god Śarva and never became tranquil.
Thus ends the first chapter named 'Description of Dakṣa's Story and the Proposal of the Purāṇa,' in the Kedārakhaṇḍa of the first Māheśvarakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Skandapurāṇa, a collection of eighty-one thousand [verses].

